{
"dur": 48.66,
"shots": [
{
"i": 1,
"start": 0.0,
"vo_start": 0.6,
"vo_end": 7.16,
"text": "On the second of January 1492, a king rode out of the most beautiful palace in Europe and handed its keys to his enemies.",
"end": 7.435
},
{
"i": 2,
"start": 7.435,
"vo_start": 7.71,
"vo_end": 14.589,
"text": "His name was Muhammad the Twelfth. The Spanish called him Boabdil. He was the last Muslim ruler in Western Europe.",
"end": 14.864
},
{
"i": 3,
"start": 14.864,
"vo_start": 15.139,
"vo_end": 24.298,
"text": "Legend says that on a mountain pass he turned for one last look at Granada and wept. And his mother told him: weep like a woman for what you could not defend like a man.",
"end": 24.573
},
{
"i": 4,
"start": 24.573,
"vo_start": 24.848,
"vo_end": 31.608,
"text": "That moment ended a story that began seven hundred and eighty-one years earlier, when a small army crossed a narrow strip of sea from Africa.",
"end": 31.883
},
{
"i": 5,
"start": 31.883,
"vo_start": 32.158,
"vo_end": 39.28,
"text": "In those centuries, the cities of Muslim Spain had street lamps, public baths and libraries, while most of Europe had none of them.",
"end": 39.555
},
{
"i": 6,
"start": 39.555,
"vo_start": 39.83,
"vo_end": 47.71,
"text": "So how did a few thousand soldiers take a whole country? How did it become the richest place in Europe? And why did it all fall apart?",
"end": 48.66
}
],
